\section{Executable footprint is not peak memory}

Start a constrained runtime design with separate budgets for flash, RAM, and response time. Where the platform permits reading bytecode directly from flash, immutable code and constant tables can remain there; writable globals, VM stacks, heap, DMA buffers, and interrupt stacks need writable storage. A compressed program may occupy little flash but require a much larger RAM image during loading. Count the maximum simultaneous usage during initialization as well as in steady state. Michael Barr and Anthony Massa's \emph{Programming Embedded Systems With C and GNU Development Tools} describes the distinct uses and constraints of flash and the role of interrupt handlers; both shape where the interpreter can run and which memory it may mutate.

Samuel Yvon and Marc Feeley's \emph{A Small Scheme VM, Compiler, and REPL in 4K} is an example of optimizing executable footprint, not of fitting an entire live workload in 4~KiB of RAM. Ribbit represents heap structures such as objects, stack cells, and instructions with fixed-size three-field records. On the C host, uniform size simplifies object scanning and avoids a per-record header; a copying collector still needs space to copy live data. Sharing a representation reduces interpreter code, but pointer chasing can cost time and data space. Compare this option with a flash-resident token stream before deciding which space budget is tighter.

\section{Worked budget: fitting copying collection}

Suppose a board has 32~KiB of RAM and a fixed partition. Reserve 4~KiB for driver buffers, 4~KiB for native and interrupt stacks, 4~KiB for VM stacks and globals, and 4~KiB for tracing, other scratch space, and margin. That leaves 16~KiB for a two-semispace heap with 8~KiB per space. A peak reachable graph of 6~KiB leaves at most 2~KiB for allocation after collection, assuming the heap arithmetic already includes all alignment and bookkeeping. A pending allocation requiring 3~KiB must fail even with every dead object removed. Treating the entire 16~KiB as usable from either side would conceal that failure.

Linker symbols or a memory map can check static reservations; high-water marks observe stacks and scratch space on tested workloads. Add a synthetic workload that retains exactly the selected live graph across a collection, then attempts one allocation that fits and one that does not. Also test the transient RAM use during program loading and peak interrupt nesting. Specify what failure does: reject a job, keep the last valid configuration, or report an error. An allocator that repeats collection forever after it has recovered no space has turned a memory boundary into a deadline failure.

