\subsection{Bound a Fixed-Width Device Event Ring}

The interrupt handoff has a memory budget even when the VM
heap is full. As a reference design, use four event records
and indices for one producer and one consumer. Events are
plain, fixed-width integers, not managed references. Reserve
one record as the gap that distinguishes full from empty.
On this toy single-core board the consumer masks the sole
producer interrupt while manipulating the queue; assume the
platform primitive also provides the required compiler ordering
and the producer cannot reenter itself. Choose drop-new on
overflow. An overflow count is saturating so a long burst does
not silently wrap it to zero.

\begin{sudocode}[Fixed storage, no interrupt-time allocation]
\Function{OnEvent}{$x$}
  \State $n\gets(tail+1)\bmod4$
  \If{$n=head$}
    \State saturating increment of $drops$ \Comment{Preserve old events}
  \Else
    \State $slot[tail]\gets x$; $tail\gets n$ \Comment{Publish last}
  \EndIf
\EndFunction
\Function{Poll}{}
  \State mask producer interrupt, preserving prior mask
  \State $r\gets$ empty
  \If{$head\ne tail$}
    \State $r\gets slot[head]$; $head\gets(head+1)\bmod4$
  \EndIf
  \State restore mask; \Return $r$
\EndFunction
\end{sudocode}

With both indices initially zero, deliver 10, 20, 30, and 40
without a poll. The first three fit and $drops=1$; 40 is lost.
Poll 10, deliver 50, and drain. The remainder is $(20,30,50)$
then empty; this case crosses the array boundary and checks
the distinction between full and empty. Measure the maximum
masked interval while taking the nonempty path. A too-long
masked section can violate a driver deadline even if the ring
never loses an event.

The records between head and tail in cyclic order are precisely
the published unread events. Both operations take constant work
and use bounded storage. Read or clear the drop counter under
the same exclusion rule. A producer on another core would not
be excluded by a local interrupt mask and needs a different
publication protocol. In either case consumers must not read an
unpublished record merely because its storage contains old bytes.

\section{Scheduling collector and device work}

An interrupt handler should do only bounded work needed to service the device: acknowledge it, record an event in preallocated storage, and leave processing to a task that can allocate. If that handoff queue fills, choose and document either backpressure where supported or a drop policy with an explicit counter. The event publication must respect the target's concurrency rules; an ordinary non-atomic flag alone is not a portable synchronization mechanism between execution contexts.

Small memory does not imply predictable time. Joshua Auerbach and colleagues' \emph{Design and Implementation of a Comprehensive Real-time Java Virtual Machine} combines ahead-of-time compilation with the Metronome collector and real-time OS support. Periodically scheduled collector work illustrates the distinction between minimizing pauses and guaranteeing mutator progress over an interval. A thin VM can avoid JIT warm-up and allocate nothing on a critical path, but other paths still need enough allocation headroom for the collector to keep up. Test latency under bursts, not just average allocation. If a device job has a $200\,\mu\mathrm{s}$ deadline, account for interference and pause costs as well as the handler's measured work. Chapter~36 of the Modern Compiler Dossier, \emph{Energy- and Thermal-Aware Compilation}, adds reasons to assess a configuration under its real duty cycle rather than on an isolated throughput loop.
